    "case_id": "90",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old woman presented with a breast lump, chronic cough, and chest pain. She had no significant medical or family history. Physical examination revealed a palpable mass in the medial outer quadrant of the right breast, along with palpable axillary and supraclavicular lymph nodes on the same side. Imaging studies, including mammography and ultrasound, identified a 2 cm nodule with an irregular border and microcalcifications, which was confirmed to be invasive ductal carcinoma via fine needle biopsy. Chest radiography and computed tomography showed multiple nodules and masses in both lungs. Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scans demonstrated increased Fluoro-deoxy-glucose (FDG) uptake in the breast cancer and axillary and supraclavicular lymph nodes, but no FDG uptake was observed in the pulmonary lesions. The patient resided in the eastern part of the country where hydatid disease is common.\n\nPositive indirect hemagglutination test.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
